[groq-gpt-oss] evals/prompts/lib/skill-p:
## TL;DR  

1. **Upgrade to MapLibre GL JS ≥ 3.0** (the version that understands an array of sprite sheets).  
2. **Add a second sprite sheet** to the style – either by editing the style JSON **or** by calling `map.addSprite()` after the style has loaded.  
3. **Give the extra sheet an `id`** (e.g. `"my-poi"`).  
4. **Reference your icons with the `id:` prefix**:  

```json
"layout": { "icon-image": "my-poi:cafe" }
```

5. Keep the basemap’s sheet as the **`default`** entry so you don’t have to touch any of its existing `icon-image` values.  

That’s it – no merging, no rebuilding, no extra network requests beyond the two PNG/JSON pairs.

Below is a step‑by‑step guide, plus a few gotchas and debugging tips.

---

## 1. Why the icons never appear

When a style has a **single** `sprite` string, MapLibre loads **one** PNG + JSON pair.  
All `icon-image` values are looked up **only** in that one index.  

If you point `sprite` at the basemap provider, the loader never even knows about the second sheet you host, so `"cafe"` (or any other name you added) is not found.  

MapLibre does **not** throw an error for a missing image; it just fires the `styleimagemissing` event and writes a single console warning the first time the ID is requested. If you never see that warning it usually means the lookup never happened because the ID was never in the index.

The fix is to tell MapLibre about **more than one sprite sheet**.

---

## 2. The feature you need: multiple sprite sheets

Since **MapLibre GL JS 3.0** (see PR #1805) the `sprite` property can be:

* a **string** (old single‑sheet form) **or**
* an **array of objects** `{ id: string, url: string }`

Each entry is treated exactly like the old string form – MapLibre will request:

```
<url>.json
<url>.png
<url>@2x.json
<url>@2x.png
```

The only difference is **how you reference the images**:

* Images in the sheet whose `id` is `"default"` are referenced **without a prefix** (the classic behaviour).  
* Images in any other sheet must be prefixed with the sheet’s `id` and a colon: `"my-poi:cafe"`.

---

## 3. Two ways to add your extra sheet

### 3.1. Edit the style JSON (preferred if you control the style)

```json
{
  "version": 8,
  "sprite": [
    {
      "id": "default",
      "url": "https://tiles.provider.com/sprites/basemap"
    },
    {
      "id": "my-poi",
      "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi"
    }
  ],
  "layers": [
    {
      "id": "poi-cafes",
      "type": "symbol",
      "source": "places",
      "source-layer": "poi",
      "layout": {
        "icon-image": "my-poi:cafe",   // <-- note the prefix
        "icon-allow-overlap": true
      }
    }
    // … other layers that still use the provider’s icons, e.g. "cafe"
  ]
}
```

**Key points**

| What | How |
|------|-----|
| **Basemap sheet** | Keep its `id` as `"default"` (or rename it to `"default"` if it wasn’t already). This lets all the existing `icon-image` values stay unchanged. |
| **Your sheet** | Choose any unique `id` (`"my-poi"` in the example). |
| **URL** | **Never** include an extension (`.png` or `.json`). MapLibre adds those automatically. |
| **Reference** | Prefix every icon name with `my-poi:`. |
| **CORS** | Both PNG and JSON must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your site’s origin). Otherwise the browser will block the request and you’ll see a CORS error in the console. |

### 3.2. Add the sheet at runtime (if you can’t edit the style file)

If the style is hosted elsewhere and you can’t change its JSON, you can still inject a second sheet **after** the style loads:

```js
// Assume you already have a MapLibre map instance called `map`
map.on('style.load', () => {
  // 1️⃣ Add the extra sprite sheet
  map.addSprite('my-poi', 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi');

  // 2️⃣ Now you can add layers that reference it
  map.addLayer({
    id: 'poi-cafes',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'places',
    'source-layer': 'poi',
    layout: {
      'icon-image': 'my-poi:cafe',
      'icon-allow-overlap': true
    }
  });
});
```

`map.addSprite(id, url)` works exactly like the array form in the style JSON: it loads `<url>.json` and `<url>.png` (and the `@2x` variants) and registers the images under the given `id`.

**Why you might still want to edit the style**  
* If you have many layers that reference your icons, editing the style once is cleaner than calling `addSprite` on every map instance.  
* `addSprite` can only be called **after** the style has loaded, so you can’t use it for layers that need the icons during the initial `load` event.

---

## 4. Full example – from scratch

Below is a minimal HTML page that demonstrates the whole flow (using the public demo basemap sprite and a tiny custom sprite you host yourself). Replace the URLs with your own.

```html
<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – multiple sprite sheets</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@3.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>#map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }</style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      // 1️⃣ Two sprite sheets: the demo basemap (default) + our custom POI sheet
      sprite: [
        { id: 'default', url: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style/sprite' },
        { id: 'my-poi',   url: 'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi' }
      ],
      sources: {
        osm: { type: 'raster', tiles: ['https://a.tile.openstreetmap.org/{z}/{x}/{y}.png'], tileSize: 256 }
      },
      layers: [
        { id: 'basemap', type: 'raster', source: 'osm' },

        // 2️⃣ A layer that uses an icon from the *default* sheet (no prefix)
        {
          id: 'city-labels',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: 'osm',
          layout: { 'text-field': 'OSM', 'text-size': 24 }
        },

        // 3️⃣ A layer that uses an icon from our custom sheet (prefix required)
        {
          id: 'my-poi-cafes',
          type: 'symbol',
          source: {
            type: 'geojson',
            data: {
              type: 'FeatureCollection',
              features: [
                { type: 'Feature', geometry: { type: 'Point', coordinates: [-0.1276, 51.5074] }, properties: {} }
              ]
            }
          },
          layout: {
            'icon-image': 'my-poi:cafe',   // <-- note the prefix
            'icon-size': 1.2,
            'icon-allow-overlap': true
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    center: [-0.1276, 51.5074],
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Optional: listen for missing‑image warnings
  map.on('styleimagemissing', e => console.warn('Missing image:', e.id));
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

*If you open this page and see the café icon, the setup works.* If you don’t, open the Network tab and verify that the two requests:

```
https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json
https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.png
```

return **200** and have the proper CORS header.

---

## 5. Common pitfalls & how to debug them

| Symptom | Likely cause | How to verify / fix |
|---------|--------------|---------------------|
| **No icons, no console warning** | The extra sheet never loaded (wrong URL, CORS, or missing `@2x` variant). | Open the Network tab → look for `…/poi.json` and `…/poi.png`. If they 404 or are blocked by CORS, fix the server. |
| **Icons still don’t appear, but you see “Image “my‑poi:cafe” could not be loaded”** | The ID is wrong (typo, missing prefix, or the sprite JSON doesn’t contain that key). | `fetch('https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi.json')` → inspect the JSON keys. Ensure the key is exactly `"cafe"` (case‑sensitive). |
| **Only the basemap icons work, my icons are invisible, but `styleimagemissing` fires** | You added the sheet **after** you added the layer, and the layer was created before the sheet finished loading. | Either (a) add the sheet **first** (`map.addSprite`) then add your layer, or (b) listen for the `data` event on the sprite (`map.on('data', e => { if (e.sourceId === 'my-poi' && e.isSourceLoaded) … })`). |
| **`map.addSprite` throws “Invalid sprite URL … must be absolute”** | You passed a URL that already includes `.json` or `.png`. | Pass the **base** URL without extension, e.g. `'https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi'`. |
| **CORS error in console** | The sprite PNG/JSON are served without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin`. | Configure your static host (S3, Netlify, Vercel, etc.) to send `*` or your site’s origin. |
| **You’re on MapLibre GL JS 2.x** | Multiple sprite sheets are not supported. | Upgrade to ≥ 3.0, or merge the sheets (the only alternative for older versions). |

**Quick test** after you think everything is set up:

```js
// After the style has loaded
console.log('Has my icon?', map.hasImage('my-poi:cafe')); // true → sheet loaded & key present
console.log('All images:', map.listImages().filter(id => id.startsWith('my-poi:')).slice(0,5));
```

If `hasImage` returns `false`, the sheet either didn’t load or the key is missing.

---

## 6. When you *must* stay on a single‑sheet style (legacy)

If you cannot upgrade to MapLibre ≥ 3.0 and you cannot edit the style JSON, you have **two** options:

1. **Merge the two sheets** into one PNG + JSON (use `spritezero`, `spreet`, or `Martin`).  
2. **Proxy the basemap’s sprite** on your own server, append your icons to the JSON, and stitch the PNGs together (still a merge, but you keep the original files untouched).

Both approaches require a build step, which you said you’d rather avoid. Upgrading is the cleanest path.

---

## 7. Recap – the minimal change you need

```json
// 1️⃣ In the style JSON (or via map.addSprite later)
"sprite": [
  { "id": "default", "url": "https://tiles.provider.com/sprites/basemap" },
  { "id": "my-poi",   "url": "https://mydomain.com/sprites/poi" }
]

// 2️⃣ In any layer that uses your icons
"layout": {
  "icon-image": "my-poi:cafe"
}
```

That’s all. No merging, no rebuilding, no extra network traffic beyond the two sprite files.

---

### Further reading

* **Style spec – `sprite`** – https://maplibre.org/maplibre-style-spec/sprite/

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste‑ready** example that works with **MapLibre GL JS 6**.

* 40 + SVG icons are fetched from your own domain, turned into `Image` objects and registered with `map.addImage()`.  
* A GeoJSON source (`venues.geojson`) contains a `category` property that matches the SVG file name (e.g. `"cafe.svg"` → id **`cafe`**).  
* A single **symbol layer** draws the points, using the runtime‑registered icons.  
* The default collision‑detection (`icon‑allow‑overlap: false`) makes icons disappear when they would overlap, so the map stays readable when you zoom out.  
* An optional `icon‑size` expression scales the icons with zoom so they are tiny at low zooms and grow larger when you zoom in.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>MapLibre – 5 000 venues with SVG icons</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <!-- MapLibre GL JS 6 -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet"/>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@6.11.2/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<script>
/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   1️⃣  Initialise the map
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const map = new maplibregl.Map({
  container: 'map',
  style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json',   // any base style you like
  center: [-122.42, 37.77],   // San Francisco (change to your area)
  zoom: 12,
  hash: true
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   2️⃣  List of icon IDs (the part before “.svg”) that you have.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
const ICON_IDS = [
  'cafe', 'restaurant', 'park', 'library', 'school',
  'hospital', 'bank', 'pharmacy', 'bar', 'museum',
  // … add the rest of your 30‑plus ids here …
];

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   3️⃣  Helper: load a single SVG as an Image element.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function loadSvgAsImage(id) {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const img = new Image(24, 24);               // set the desired pixel size
    img.crossOrigin = 'anonymous';               // required for cross‑origin SVGs
    img.onload  = () => resolve({ id, img });
    img.onerror = (e) => reject(new Error(`SVG "${id}" failed to load`));
    img.src = `https://your‑domain.example.com/icons/${id}.svg`;
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   4️⃣  Load *all* icons, then register them with the map.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
async function registerIcons() {
  const promises = ICON_IDS.map(loadSvgAsImage);
  const results   = await Promise.all(promises);

  for (const {id, img} of results) {
    // Guard against a name clash with a sprite image that might already exist
    if (!map.hasImage(id)) {
      // `sdf: false` (default) – the SVG keeps its colours.
      map.addImage(id, img, { sdf: false });
    } else {
      console.warn(`Image id "${id}" already exists in the style – skipping`);
    }
  }
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   5️⃣  Add the GeoJSON source (5 000 venues) and the symbol layer.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
function addVenueLayer() {
  // 5 000 points – you can host the file yourself or fetch it from an API.
  map.addSource('venues', {
    type: 'geojson',
    data: 'https://your‑domain.example.com/data/venues.geojson'
  });

  map.addLayer({
    id: 'venue-icons',
    type: 'symbol',
    source: 'venues',
    // The `category` property must match one of the icon IDs above.
    layout: {
      'icon-image': ['get', 'category'],   // e.g. "cafe", "park", …
      // Keep the default collision handling (icons disappear when they overlap)
      // If you want them to *always* draw, set "icon-allow-overlap": true.
      // Scale icons with zoom – optional but makes low‑zoom maps cleaner.
      'icon-size': [
        'interpolate',
        ['linear'],
        ['zoom'],
        10, 0.4,   // tiny at zoom 10
        14, 0.8,   // medium at zoom 14
        18, 1.2    // full size at zoom 18
      ]
    },
    paint: {
      // If you ever use SDF icons you could colour them here.
      // 'icon-color': '#ff6600',
    }
  });
}

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   6️⃣  Wire everything together.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('load', async () => {
  try {
    await registerIcons();   // 1️⃣ load & add all SVGs
    addVenueLayer();         // 2️⃣ add source + layer
    console.log('✅ All icons loaded and venues rendered');
  } catch (e) {
    console.error('❌ Something went wrong while loading icons:', e);
  }
});

/* --------------------------------------------------------------
   7️⃣  Optional: show a helpful message when an icon is missing.
   -------------------------------------------------------------- */
map.on('styleimagemissing', (e) => {
  console.warn(`⚠️  Image "${e.id}" is not in any sprite sheet nor added via addImage`);
});
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Loads a base style (any style works). | Gives you a map canvas and the GL context. |
| **2️⃣ List of icon IDs** | You enumerate the 40 + categories you have SVGs for. | The IDs are the strings you’ll use in `icon-image`. |
| **3️⃣ `loadSvgAsImage`** | Creates an `Image` element, sets `crossOrigin='anonymous'`, and resolves when the SVG finishes loading. | `map.addImage()` expects an `HTMLImageElement` (or `ImageData`). |
| **4️⃣ `registerIcons`** | Calls `loadSvgAsImage` for every ID, then registers each with `map.addImage(id, img)`. | Runtime images become part of the style’s image registry, just like sprites. |
| **5️⃣ Add source & layer** | Adds the GeoJSON source (`venues`) and a single symbol layer that reads the `category` property for the icon name. | One layer is enough; the expression `['get','category']` picks the right icon per feature. |
| **Collision handling** | The default (`icon-allow-overlap: false`) hides icons that would overlap, so the map stays legible when zoomed out. | No extra code needed – this is the built‑in “pile‑up‑prevention”. |
| **Zoom‑dependent size** (optional) | `icon-size` interpolates between 0.4 × and 1.2 × the SVG’s intrinsic size. | Small icons at low zoom keep the map uncluttered; they grow when you zoom in. |
| **6️⃣ Wire‑up** | Waits for the map’s `load` event, then loads icons **before** adding the layer. | Guarantees every `icon-image` reference already exists. |
| **7️⃣ `styleimagemissing` listener** | Logs a warning if a feature asks for an icon that wasn’t registered. | Helpful debugging if a `category` value doesn’t match any SVG. |

---

#### Things to keep in mind

* **CORS** – The SVG files must be served with `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or your site’s origin). Setting `crossOrigin = 'anonymous'` on the `Image` forces the browser to check CORS; otherwise `addImage` will throw.
* **Icon dimensions** – The `new Image(24,24)` call sets the *intrinsic* pixel size. Adjust the numbers (or omit them) to match the visual size you want before the `icon-size` expression scales them.
* **Performance** – 40 × SVGs is tiny; they are fetched once on map load. If you ever need **hundreds** of icons you might prefer a sprite sheet, but for a few dozen runtime images this approach is simplest.
* **Changing the icon set later** – Call `map.removeImage(id)` first if you need to replace an existing image; `addImage` will otherwise throw an error.

That’s it! Drop the HTML into a file, replace the placeholder URLs (`your‑domain.example.com/...`) with your real endpoints, and you’ll see 5 000 venues each rendered with the correct SVG icon, automatically de‑duplicated when they would otherwise overlap. Enjoy your map.

### TL;DR  
The current test

```js
layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&
layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'
```

is **not a reliable way to recognise a route‑shield layer**.  
Both plain place‑name layers and many other point‑symbol layers satisfy those two conditions, so you end up flagging the wrong things and missing the real shields.

A shield layer has a *different* set of clues:

| What a shield layer **does** | Why it matters |
|------------------------------|----------------|
| **Has an `icon-image` that points to a badge** (e.g. `road_3`, `us-interstate_2`, `my-icons:shield-badge`) | The badge is the background of the shield; plain POI layers usually use a simple pictogram (`cafe`, `park`, …) that never contains a numeric suffix. |
| **Uses `icon-text-fit` (or `icon-text-fit` ≠ `"none"`).** | Only stretchable shields need the text‑fit logic; ordinary point symbols leave the default (`"none"`). |
| **Provides a `text-field` that contains the route reference** (`"{ref}"`, `"{ref}"`, `"{network}_{ref}"`, etc.) | A place‑name layer either has no `text-field` or it contains a name (`"{name}"`). |
| **Often sets `icon-text-fit-padding`** (or at least one of the padding values) | The padding is required for the badge to grow around the text. |
| **Usually has `icon-size` (or a zoom‑function) that scales with the length of the reference** | Shields are often larger than ordinary icons. |
| **May have a non‑default sprite prefix** (`"my-icons:road_2"`). | The heuristic must look past the default sprite. |
| **`text-anchor` is usually left at its default (`"center"`), but that alone is not a discriminator** | The original heuristic relied on the *absence* of `text‑anchor`; that is true for *all* symbol layers that don’t override it. |

In short, **the presence of a badge‑type `icon-image` together with a non‑default `icon-text-fit` and a route‑reference `text-field` is the signature of a shield**.

---

## Why the current heuristic fails

```js
layer.layout['text-anchor'] === undefined &&   // true for *any* symbol layer that doesn’t override it
layer.layout['symbol-placement'] === 'point'   // true for most point‑symbol layers (places, POIs, etc.)
```

* `text-anchor` defaults to `"center"`; most layers never set it, so the test is true for the vast majority of point symbols.  
* `symbol-placement: "point"` is the default for *all* point‑symbol layers, not just shields.  

Consequently:

* **Place‑name layers** (`icon-image: "city"` + `text-field: "{name}"`) satisfy the test → false positive.  
* **Shield layers that explicitly set `text-anchor`** (some styles do this to align the text inside the badge) or that use `symbol-placement: "line"` (the “road‑label” variant that draws shields along a line) are missed → false negative.

---

## A more robust heuristic

Below is a **minimal, style‑agnostic** function that captures the typical shield pattern while staying tolerant of variations (different sprite IDs, custom badge names, stretchable vs. pre‑drawn badges).

```js
/**
 * Returns true if a symbol layer looks like a route‑shield layer.
 *
 * @param {object} layer – a layer object from a MapLibre style
 * @returns {boolean}
 */
function isShieldLayer(layer) {
  if (layer.type !== 'symbol') return false;

  const layout = layer.layout || {};

  // 1️⃣ Must have an icon‑image that looks like a badge.
  //    • pre‑drawn badges: road_1 … road_6, us-interstate_2, …
  //    • custom badges often contain “shield”, “badge”, or end with a numeric suffix
  const icon = layout['icon-image'];
  if (!icon) return false;

  // Strip any sprite‑prefix (e.g. "my-icons:road_2")
  const iconName = icon.includes(':') ? icon.split(':')[1] : icon;

  const badgePattern = /^(?:road|us-(?:interstate|highway|state)|default|shield|badge)[_-]\d+$/i;
  if (!badgePattern.test(iconName)) return false;

  // 2️⃣ Must be using text‑fit (stretchable badge) OR have a pre‑drawn badge
  //    (pre‑drawn badges also have a numeric suffix, so the pattern above already catches them)
  const textFit = layout['icon-text-fit'];
  const usesTextFit = textFit && textFit !== 'none';

  // 3️⃣ Must have a text field that references a route ref (or network+ref)
  const textField = layout['text-field'];
  if (!textField) return false;

  // Accept common expressions: "{ref}", "{network}_{ref}", "{ref}" with concat, etc.
  const refPattern = /\{(?:ref|network|ref_length|ref_number)\}/i;
  if (!refPattern.test(textField)) return false;

  // 4️⃣ (Optional but helpful) – shields usually have a non‑default icon‑size or a zoom function
  //    This helps to weed out tiny POI icons that accidentally match the badge pattern.
  const iconSize = layout['icon-size'];
  if (iconSize !== undefined) {
    // If it is a constant number, it should be > 0.5 (most POIs are <0.5)
    if (typeof iconSize === 'number' && iconSize < 0.5) return false;
    // If it is an expression, we assume it’s intentional for a shield.
  }

  // 5️⃣ Symbol‑placement can be "point" *or* "line". Shields are drawn on lines in many styles.
  //    We accept either, but we keep the original “point” check as a fallback.
  const placement = layout['symbol-placement'];
  if (placement && placement !== 'point' && placement !== 'line') return false;

  // If we got here, the layer looks like a shield.
  return true;
}
```

### Why this works

| Check | What it catches | What it filters out |
|-------|----------------|---------------------|
| **`icon-image` pattern** | Pre‑drawn badge IDs (`road_3`, `us-interstate_2`) **and** custom badge names (`shield-badge`, `my-icons:badge_4`). | Plain POI icons (`cafe`, `park`, `city`) that lack a numeric suffix or badge‑related keyword. |
| **`icon-text-fit`** | Guarantees the layer is using the *stretchable* badge technique (common for shields that need to grow with the ref length). | POI symbols that never set `icon-text-fit`. |
| **`text-field` contains `{ref}` / `{network}`** | Shields always label the route number; place‑name layers use `{name}` or a custom field. | POI layers that label a name instead of a ref. |
| **`icon-size` sanity check** (optional) | Shields are usually drawn at a size ≥ 0.5 – 1.0; tiny icons are likely POIs. | Very small icons that accidentally match the badge pattern (e.g. a custom “road_1” POI). |
| **`symbol-placement`** | Accepts both `"point"` (most OSM‑Bright/Liberty shields) **and** `"line"` (some styles draw shields along the road line). | Excludes layers that deliberately place symbols on polygons or other geometry. |

You can tighten or loosen the regexes depending on the exact sprite set you use. The key is **looking at the image name and the presence of a route‑reference text field**, not at the absence of `text-anchor`.

---

## Quick sanity‑check against common styles

| Style | Typical shield layer definition | `isShieldLayer` result |
|-------|--------------------------------|------------------------|
| **OSM Bright** | `icon-image: "road_3"`<br>`icon-text-fit: "both"`<br>`text-field: "{ref}"` | ✅ true |
| **OSM Liberty** | `icon-image: "default_2"`<br>`icon-text-fit: "both"`<br>`text-field: "{ref}"` | ✅ true |
| **OpenMapTiles (US)** | `icon-image: "us-interstate_2"`<br>`text-field: "{ref}"` (no `icon-text-fit`) | ✅ true (badge pattern matches) |
| **Place‑name layer** | `icon-image: "city"`<br>`text-field: "{name}"` | ❌ false |
| **POI layer** | `icon-image: "cafe"`<br>`text-field: "{name}"` | ❌ false |
| **Custom point label** | `icon-image: "marker"` (no numeric suffix) | ❌ false |

---

## How to integrate it into your audit script

```js
style.layers.forEach(layer => {
  if (isShieldLayer(layer)) {
    // Mark as shield
    layer._auditTag = 'shield';
  } else if (layer.type === 'symbol' && layer.layout && layer.layout['text-field']) {
    // Probably a plain label / POI
    layer._auditTag = 'label-or-poi';
  }
});
```

If you need to **differentiate pre‑drawn badges from stretchable ones**, you can add a second flag:

```js
layer._shieldType = (layout['icon-text-fit'] && layout['icon-text-fit'] !== 'none')
  ? 'stretchable'
  : 'pre-drawn';
```

---

## Edge Cases & Things to Keep in Mind

1. **Multiple sprite sheets** – If your style uses the array form (`sprite: [{id:'default',url:…},{id:'my-icons',url:…}]`), the `icon-image` may be prefixed (`"my-icons:road_2"`). The regex above strips the prefix before testing, so it works unchanged.

2. **Custom badge naming** – Some projects use names like `"shield_us_i"` or `"badge-5"`. Extend the `badgePattern` to include those keywords (`shield|badge|shield_` etc.) or maintain a whitelist of known badge IDs from the sprite JSON (`Object.keys(spriteJson).filter(id => /_?\d+$/.test(id))`).

3. **Missing sprite** – If the sprite sheet fails to load, `map.hasImage(id)` will be `false`. In a static audit (no map instance) you can read the sprite JSON directly and verify that the `icon-image` you detected actually exists.

4. **Future style changes** – The MapLibre spec may add a dedicated `icon-type: "shield"` property someday. Until then, the combination of **badge‑style `icon-image` + `icon-text-fit` + route‑ref `text-field`** is the most reliable heuristic.

---

### Bottom line

*The heuristic based on `text-anchor` and `symbol-placement` is too generic.*  
A shield layer is best identified by **what it draws** (a badge image) **and what it labels** (a route reference). Check the `icon-image` name (numeric suffix or badge keyword), the presence of `icon-text-fit` (or a pre‑drawn badge pattern), and a `text-field` that references `{ref}` (or `{network}_{ref}`). Using those three signals together will correctly flag real shields and ignore ordinary place‑name or POI layers.

Below is a **complete, copy‑and‑paste ready** example that

1. **Creates a MapLibre GL JS map** (you can swap the style URL for your own).  
2. **Adds a draggable pin (Marker) at a given longitude/latitude.**  
3. **Shows a Popup when the user clicks the pin** (the popup also follows the pin while it’s being dragged).  
4. (Optional) **Logs the new coordinate after each drag** – useful if you need to store the location.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <title>Draggable Pin with Popup – MapLibre GL JS</title>
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">

  <!-- MapLibre GL JS CSS -->
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />

  <style>
    body { margin:0; padding:0; }
    #map { position:absolute; top:0; bottom:0; width:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>

<div id="map"></div>

<!-- MapLibre GL JS -->
<script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.2.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>

<script>
  // -------------------------------------------------
  // 1️⃣ Initialise the map
  // -------------------------------------------------
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',                     // id of the div above
    style: 'https://demotiles.maplibre.org/style.json', // any valid style URL
    center: [-122.42, 37.77],             // initial view (lon, lat)
    zoom: 12
  });

  // Wait until the style and sources are ready before adding anything else
  map.on('load', () => {
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 2️⃣ Create a Popup (but don’t open it yet)
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const popup = new maplibregl.Popup({
      offset: 25,               // distance from the marker tip
      closeButton: true,
      closeOnClick: false      // keep it open when the map is clicked
    }).setHTML(`
      <h4>My draggable pin</h4>
      <p>Click the marker to open this popup.</p>
      <p id="coords"></p>
    `);

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 3️⃣ Create a draggable Marker at a known coordinate
    // -------------------------------------------------
    const startLngLat = [-122.42, 37.77]; // ← change to whatever you need

    const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({
      draggable: true,          // <‑‑ makes the pin draggable
      color: '#ff0000'          // optional – default is the default pin image
    })
      .setLngLat(startLngLat)
      .setPopup(popup)          // associate the popup with the marker
      .addTo(map);              // finally add it to the map

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 4️⃣ Open the popup when the user clicks the marker
    // -------------------------------------------------
    // The Marker already knows to open its attached popup on a click,
    // but if you want to control it yourself you can also listen:
    marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', () => {
      popup.addTo(map);               // ensures the popup is attached to the map
      popup.setLngLat(marker.getLngLat()); // keep it anchored to the marker
    });

    // -------------------------------------------------
    // 5️⃣ (Optional) React to drag events – e.g. update UI or store the new point
    // -------------------------------------------------
    function updateCoordinates() {
      const lngLat = marker.getLngLat();
      document.getElementById('coords').textContent =
        `Current position: ${lngLat.lng.toFixed(5)}, ${lngLat.lat.toFixed(5)}`;
    }

    // Show the coordinates the first time
    updateCoordinates();

    // When dragging ends, update the displayed coordinates
    marker.on('dragend', () => {
      updateCoordinates();
    });
  });
</script>

</body>
</html>
```

### How it works, step‑by‑step

| Step | What it does | Key API |
|------|--------------|----------|
| **1️⃣ Initialise the map** | Creates a `maplibregl.Map` instance. | `new maplibregl.Map({...})` |
| **2️⃣ Create a Popup** | Builds a `Popup` object that will be shown when the marker is clicked. | `new maplibregl.Popup({...})` |
| **3️⃣ Create a draggable Marker** | `Marker` receives `{draggable:true}`; you can also set a custom colour or an HTML element (`element:`). | `new maplibregl.Marker({...})` |
| **4️⃣ Wire the click → popup** | `marker.setPopup(popup)` automatically opens the popup on a click. The explicit `click` listener shown is optional but useful if you need extra logic. | `marker.setPopup()`, `marker.getElement().addEventListener('click', …)` |
| **5️⃣ React to drag events** | `dragend` fires once the user releases the pin. `marker.getLngLat()` returns the new coordinate. | `marker.on('dragend', …)` |

### Customising the pin

* **Custom image** – instead of the default pin you can pass an HTML element or an image URL:

  ```js
  const el = document.createElement('div');
  el.style.backgroundImage = 'url(https://example.com/my-pin.svg)';
  el.style.width = '32px';
  el.style.height = '32px';
  const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ element: el, draggable: true })
      .setLngLat([lon, lat])
      .addTo(map);
  ```

* **Different colours** – `color: '#00ff00'` works for the built‑in pin; for a custom image you’d change the image itself.

* **Popup behaviour** – `closeOnClick: false` keeps the popup open when the user clicks elsewhere; `offset` moves the popup away from the tip of the pin.

### Common pitfalls & how to avoid them

| Issue | Why it happens | Fix |
|-------|----------------|-----|
| **Popup never appears** | The marker was added **before** the map’s `load` event, or the popup wasn’t attached to the map. | Add the marker (and `setPopup`) inside `map.on('load', …)`. |
| **Marker jumps to a different place after drag** | The map’s `center` or `zoom` is being programmatically reset elsewhere. | Ensure no other code calls `map.setCenter`/`map.jumpTo` after the drag. |
| **Cross‑origin SVGs fail** | `Marker` uses an HTML element; if you load an SVG via `loadImage`/`addImage` you need CORS headers. | Host the SVG on a domain that sends `Access‑Control‑Allow‑Origin: *` (or the same origin). |
| **Pin disappears at high zoom** | The default pin is a raster PNG; at very high DPI it can look blurry. | Use a vector‑friendly SVG element (see “Custom image” above) or supply a `@2x` sprite if you go the sprite route. |

---

#### TL;DR

```js
const marker = new maplibregl.Marker({ draggable: true })
  .setLngLat([lon, lat])
  .setPopup(new maplibregl.Popup().setHTML('Hello!'))
  .addTo(map);
```

That one‑liner (inside `map.on('load')`) gives you a **draggable pin** that **opens a popup on click**. The longer example above shows how to keep the popup anchored, display the current coordinates, and customise the look. Happy mapping!
